<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagriamel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1960s–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagriamel is a political and customary movement founded on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-santo">Espiritu Santo</a> in the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> in the mid-1960s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-stevens">Jimmy Stevens</a> and Chief Paul Buluk. It demanded the return of uncultivated land claimed by European planters and defended customary ways of life. Backed by some French settlers and American business interests, it opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuaaku-pati">Vanua'aku Pati</a> and led the failed secession of Espiritu Santo in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagriamel.jpg" alt="Nagriamel" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vectorized by Froztbyte · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vemerana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Nagriamel, also known as Vemarana or the Nagriamel Customs Union, was established in January 1966 at a meeting of chiefs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-santo">Espiritu Santo</a> called by Chief Buluk of Big Bay. Its name joins those of two plants, nagria, a croton, and mel, a cycas. It was the first political movement to reach across the whole of the New Hebrides, and by 1969 it was thought to have around 10,000 members, about one in eight of the population, mostly in the north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-stevens">Jimmy Stevens</a> used much of the money given to the movement to build an agricultural complex at Vanafo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nagriamel called for the return of all European-owned land not used for farming and held that the New Hebrides was not ready for independence and the modernisation it would bring; it was reputedly manipulated by anti-independence French factions and the Phoenix Foundation. It won two of the 29 elected seats of the new Representative Assembly in 1975.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-lini">Walter Lini</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuaaku-pati">Vanua'aku Pati</a> led the drive to independence, Nagriamel sought to delay the end of the British-French condominium, and on the eve of independence in 1980 it proclaimed the Republic of Vemerana on Santo. Vanua'aku Pati forces and soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> put down the new state weeks later, and reprisals against its supporters spread through the northern islands.</p><p>After independence the party won one seat in 1983, lost it in 1987 and held one from 1991 until 1998. Havo Moli regained a seat in 2008 and became Minister for Agriculture; the party won three seats in 2012, kept them in 2016, and was reduced to one in 2020.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its programme joined support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>'s status as a tax haven and for private enterprise with free education and health care, and opposition to modern individualism as a threat to customary values. John Lum became its president in 2024 and won its only seat in the 2025 election, after which, under the constitutional referendum of 2024, he affiliated with the Land and Justice Party.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagriamel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Vanuaʻaku Pati under Walter Lini led the drive for independence, opposed by the <b>Nagriamel</b> movement of Jimmy Stevens, which attempted secession on Espiritu Santo in 1980.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-hebrides-condominium">New Hebrides Condominium</a></li></ul></section>
